= Machine consciousness (technical) =

== Question decomposition ==
Separate: (1) functional consciousness, such as reportable global access; (2) phenomenal consciousness; (3) computational architectures thought relevant under a specified theory; and (4) evidence that an implementation satisfies a theory's conditions. No inference from benchmark scores alone establishes (2).

== Theory-contingent indicators ==
Butlin et al. (2023) derive indicators from recurrent processing, global workspace, higher-order, predictive-processing and attention-schema theories. Later work proposes evaluating the evidential contribution of specific indicators while preserving uncertainty. The candidate properties are not a unified checklist whose sum proves consciousness; theory confirmation, implementation fidelity, and alternative causal explanations remain open.

== Tests and confounds ==
* Self-reports can be shaped by training and prompting; do not equate declarations with first-person ground truth.
* Internal recurrence, memory, or global data routing can have purely functional explanations.
* A system may have stable operational identity without subjective experience.
* Absence of an indicator tied to one theory is not a universal disproof under competing theories.

== Research protocol ==
Describe the physical and computational architecture; select theories explicitly; derive falsifiable implementation-level predictions; measure candidate mechanisms; compare against simpler non-conscious models; report uncertainty rather than binary certification. These are methodology recommendations, not validated evidence of a particular model's experience.

